.TH TCL 1 "Riplix" "Ripple"
.SH NAME
tcl \- run the Picol Tcl subset
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
tcl [-c script | file [arguments ...]]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run a script, evaluate -c text, or enter an interactive Tcl-style prompt.
The command names picol, tcl and tclsh select the same interpreter.
.PP
Supported facilities include variables, procedures, lists, expr, if, while,
for, foreach, catch, source, file channels, flat dictionaries and exec. This is a small Picol
based subset, not the full Tcl language or package ecosystem.
.PP
open filename a appends to a text file. Read it later with cat filename.
exec runs a command with literal arguments and captures its standard output.
The trailing arguments >@stdout 2>@stderr instead stream output directly;
use this form for build logs. A failed child sets errorCode to a list beginning
with CHILDSTATUS or CHILDKILLED, followed by its PID and exit status or signal.
.PP
Use {*}$list to expand a list into command arguments. env(NAME) variables
read and update the environment inherited by child processes. Braced expr
expressions support command substitutions, with short-circuit evaluation.
.PP
Build helpers include pwd, cd, glob (with -nocomplain), file join, dirname,
tail, mkdir, rename, delete, exists, isfile, isdirectory, mtime and size.
mkdir creates parent directories. rename rejects an existing destination.
delete removes files and empty directories; its -force option only ignores
missing paths and does not recursively remove directory trees.
dict supports create, get, set, exists, size and keys for flat dictionaries.
.PP
Strings and captured output are limited to 30,000 characters; lists and
expanded commands to 1,024 words. exec also shares the system's 4,096-character
argument/environment budget, including pointer vectors and terminators.
Ctrl-C interrupts and returns to the shell; exit leaves the interpreter.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tcl /hello.tcl Ripple
cat /tcl-visits
tcl -c 'puts [expr {6 * 7}]'
.fi
